            <ArticleTitle>Infected bronchogenic cyst causing dysphagia and retrosternal pain.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>e13-4</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Bronchogenic cysts are congenital. They are typically discovered in infancy or early childhood. Secondary infection of the cyst is uncommon. We present the case of a 17-year-old female who presented to the emergency department with intermediate onset of upper abdominal, and retrosternal chest pain and fever. Subsequent X-ray and computerised tomography scan showed a bronchogenic cyst. The patient underwent subacute thoracotomy where a bronchogenic cyst filled with pus was located and excised. Bronchogenic cysts can be a rare cause of retrosternal pain.</AbstractText>
